Yes — before you give Copilot/Claude the real-world task, I would do a small setup/verification step.

You do NOT need to manually feed all 20 repos into Claude's context.

The important thing is that ContextPilot itself can see/index those 20 repos and that Copilot can invoke its MCP tools.

Your workspace should look roughly like:

my-workspace/
│
├── repo-1/
├── repo-2/
├── repo-3/
├── ...
├── repo-20/
│
└── contextpilot/
    ├── src/
    ├── package.json
    └── ...


1. FIRST — MAKE SURE CONTEXTPILOT IS HEALTHY

From the contextpilot directory:

npm install
npm test
npm run build

You already had 24/24 tests passing in Phase 1, so after Phase 3, just make sure the tests/build still pass.

Then test the MCP server itself, if the project has the expected script:

npm run start

or whatever package.json defines for the MCP server.

Don't leave a terminal window running it manually if you've configured VS Code to start it. VS Code can manage/start MCP servers for you.


2. CONNECT CONTEXTPILOT TO COPILOT

Since you're using GitHub Copilot + Claude inside VS Code, you want Copilot Agent to see ContextPilot's MCP tools.

Create:

my-workspace/
└── .vscode/
    └── mcp.json

Something along these lines:

{
  "servers": {
    "contextpilot": {
      "type": "stdio",
      "command": "node",
      "args": [
        "${workspaceFolder}/contextpilot/dist/mcp/server.js"
      ]
    }
  }
}

IMPORTANT:
The exact path/command should match what Claude built in Phase 1–3.

Don't blindly use dist/mcp/server.js if the actual build output is different.


3. VERIFY COPILOT ACTUALLY SEES CONTEXTPILOT

Open VS Code:

Copilot Chat → Agent mode → Configure Tools

You should see something similar to:

MCP Servers
  contextpilot
      analyze_repository
      index_repository
      search_code
      find_symbol
      dependency_graph
      find_relevant_files
      optimize_context
      estimate_tokens
      ...

The exact list depends on what you've implemented so far.

If you see those tools, you're ready.


4. DO NOT TELL CLAUDE TO READ ALL 20 REPOS

This is the key distinction.

Don't give Copilot a prompt like:

"Read all 20 repositories so you understand the workspace."

That defeats the entire purpose of ContextPilot.

Instead, ContextPilot should have access to:

20 repositories
      ↓
Workspace discovery
      ↓
Repository indexes
      ↓
Workspace Graph
      ↓
Relevant repositories
      ↓
Relevant projects
      ↓
Relevant files
      ↓
Relevant symbols
      ↓
Minimum sufficient context

So Claude's actual context might eventually look like:

Task:
"Add X to the retirement distribution API"

ContextPilot determines:

Repositories:
  repo-4
  repo-7
  repo-12

Projects:
  Distribution.Api
  Distribution.Core
  Customer.Client

Files:
  DistributionController.cs
  DistributionService.cs
  DistributionResponse.cs
  CustomerClient.cs
  DistributionServiceTests.cs

Symbols:
  DistributionService
  DistributionResponse
  ICustomerClient

Instead of Claude independently exploring:

20 repos
→ 300 projects
→ 8,000 files
→ thousands of irrelevant files

That's the experiment we actually want to test.


5. DO ONE TEST BEFORE GIVING IT THE REAL TASK

Don't give it your actual business task yet.

Give Copilot/Claude this:

"ContextPilot validation test

You have access to the entire workspace, which contains approximately 20 repositories.

Do not modify any files.

Use ContextPilot to index/discover the workspace and determine:

1. How many repositories are present.
2. What projects exist in each repository.
3. Which repositories appear to have relationships/dependencies with each other.
4. Show me how ContextPilot determines relevant repositories/files.
5. Pick one representative repository and demonstrate find_relevant_files and optimize_context.
6. Report the estimated token count before and after optimization.

Do not solve or implement any business task yet.

The purpose of this exercise is to validate ContextPilot's workspace discovery, indexing, ranking, dependency analysis, and context optimization.

Do not load the entire source code of all repositories into the conversation. Use ContextPilot's tools to progressively narrow the context."

What you want to see is something like:

Workspace
  ├── 20 repositories discovered
  │
  ├── repo-A
  │     ├── 5 projects
  │     └── 214 files
  │
  ├── repo-B
  │     ├── 3 projects
  │     └── 147 files
  │
  ...

ContextPilot selected:

Repositories:
  repo-A
  repo-F

Files:
  11 selected
  37 excluded

Estimated context:
  Before: ~48,000 tokens
  After:  ~9,500 tokens

Reduction: ~80% estimated

The important word is "estimated."

Don't claim that this is actual Claude token/billing reduction yet.


6. ONE MORE THING I'D CHANGE BEFORE THE REAL-WORLD TEST

Because you have 20 repos, I would tell Claude to treat the workspace as a workspace-level ContextPilot environment, not just 20 independent repositories.

Give Claude this instruction before the validation test:

"The workspace contains approximately 20 related repositories. ContextPilot must treat the workspace as a multi-repository environment.

Do not assume repositories are independent.

Discover cross-repository relationships through solution/project references, NuGet/package dependencies, namespaces, imports, configuration, Git metadata, APIs, and other locally available signals.

Maintain repository-level indexes as well as workspace-level relationships.

When analyzing a task, first identify potentially relevant repositories, then narrow to projects, files, symbols, dependencies, tests, and configuration.

Never load all repository source code simply because it is available.

Optimize for minimum sufficient context rather than maximum available context.

Do not modify any of the 20 existing repositories during this validation.

ContextPilot itself may be modified as necessary."


7. THE SEQUENCE I RECOMMEND

Right now:

1. Finish Phase 3
        ↓
2. npm test / npm build
        ↓
3. Configure ContextPilot MCP in VS Code
        ↓
4. Verify tools appear in Copilot Agent
        ↓
5. Give Claude the validation prompt
        ↓
6. Inspect results
        ↓
7. Fix anything wrong
        ↓
8. THEN give it your real-world task

And importantly, you don't need to manually run ContextPilot every time once MCP is configured.

VS Code can start/manage the local MCP server.

The goal is:

Copilot Agent
      ↓
ContextPilot MCP
      ↓
Workspace Index
      ↓
20 repositories
      ↓
Ranking / Dependency Analysis
      ↓
Minimum sufficient context
      ↓
Claude


So the short answer is:

NO — you should not manually run ContextPilot and paste its results into Claude.

YES — you should make sure ContextPilot builds/tests successfully and is configured as an MCP server in your VS Code workspace.

YES — you should verify Copilot can see the ContextPilot tools.

YES — run one read-only validation test against your 20-repo workspace before giving Claude the real business task.

That validation will tell us whether ContextPilot is actually selecting the right context before we use it on a real development task.
